    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d544">

                <pb facs="0729" n="639" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg639"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94/926: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-03-26T13:00:00+08:00">March 26, 1934—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 26—7:37 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>139. The following telegram has been received from Peck<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d544-f1"/> at Nanking: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“March 24, 1 p.m. What follows is translation of informal comments made
                            to the press by the Foreign Office March 23d and published today.</p>
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                            <p>‘The nature of the correspondence exchanged between the United States
                                and Japan is informal. Until the full texts have been seen no
                                definite comments can be made. So far as can be observed up to the
                                present the motive for the exchange of correspondence is probably to
                                mitigate the tense atmosphere between the two countries during the
                                past 2 years. There is no other object involved. This view is
                                supported by the circular telegram of the American Department of
                                State to its Legation and Consulates abroad<anchor n="14"
                                    xml:id="d544-f2"/> in which it is stated that between the United
                                States and Japan “No negotiations were conducted in the past and no
                                negotiations are being conducted now.”</p>
                            <pb facs="0730" n="640" xml:id="pg640"/>
                            <p>With regard to the nonrecognition of the puppet organization the
                                American Government has repeatedly declared this to be its unchanged
                                policy. On June 7, 1933 the Advisory Committee of the League of
                                Nations adopted a resolution<anchor n="15" xml:id="d544-f3"/>
                                relative to nonrecognition of the puppet state. The American
                                Minister in Switzerland addressed a formal report to the Secretary
                                General of the League expressing agreement.<anchor n="16"
                                    xml:id="d544-f4"/> We firmly believe that the American
                                Government will certainly continue its past policy and will not
                                recognize the puppet organization.’”</p>
                        </quote>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d544-f1" n="13" xml:id="d544-f1-content">
                <p>Willys R. Peck, Counselor of Legation and Consul General at Nanking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d544-f2" n="14" xml:id="d544-f2-content">
                <p>See telegram of March 21, 5 p.m., to the Ambassador in Japan, <ref
                        target="frus1931-41v01#pg_129"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >i</hi>, p. 129</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d544-f3" n="15" xml:id="d544-f3-content">
                <p>See League of Nations, <hi rendition="#italic">Official Journal</hi>, Special
                    Supplement No. 113, p. 10.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d544-f4" n="16" xml:id="d544-f4-content">
                <p>For the Department’s instructions, see No. 2319, September 20, 1933, to the
                    Chargé in Switzerland, <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_121"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 121</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
